y-id 6678 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522}] :topic/title "General Solutions of First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672875 :question-generator/id #uuid "ff33eaa5-573d-5d81-96ae-0be6c7033952" :question-generator/script "generators/69/693c8cfb9fad7a2f463430dcdb63d06593ec1b5b90cfb1410378472b5cb0c1c3/17592186229181.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ea8df2-9a57-5710-b7c3-195b889f7f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501754} {:answer/id #uuid "dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501910} {:answer/id #uuid "e796a362-8cfa-568a-9c64-27bf196de4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5+16.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501932} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d824a3-01e9-5812-b58b-a58e01e5be71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501933} {:answer/id #uuid "f48f1195-a2b9-5291-9be2-abc8416f3fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=6-22.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd85ac5a-dd49-59af-aa19-5e740d5694bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501910} :answer-field/id #uuid "3214b49d-a843-5dfc-aaf4-826626ec3984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228561}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "872cac41-3a52-503a-bac8-64cc582ee5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339385" :question/problem "A wastewater basin initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of pollutant in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which pollutant enters the basin minus the rate at which pollutant leaves the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the pollutant enters the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *pollutant* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the basin.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the basin remains $750\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of pollutant in the basin, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *pollutant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =22.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=22.5.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+22.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+22.5 & =6 \\\\ A & =-16.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1475d2-7658-5bfe-ac43-ffd1505c10be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.83kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228568} {:answer/id #uuid "f373892c-d44d-5ca4-87c6-9686668b6bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.02kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228569} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a3f476-7ca4-5e94-a291-5155c9a0a5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.11kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228570} {:answer/id #uuid "190a0242-051d-533b-8e18-6d0b767d11ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.45kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228571} {:answer/id #uuid "53ddf027-12e1-5680-907f-e7217bcbea8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.76kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190a0242-051d-533b-8e18-6d0b767d11ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.45kg" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228571} :answer-field/id #uuid "60eabee9-a505-5182-8327-f4aaf6a75e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228567}] :question/id #uuid "0b497833-a441-5b14-bcbb-b0d828ec78fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339392" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $900L$ of water containing $10kg$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The volume stays $900\\,\\mathrm{L}$. The salt inflow is $0.02(30)=0.6\\,\\mathrm{kg/min}$ and outflow is $30Q/900=Q/30$. Thus $Q′+Q/30=0.6$. With $Q(0)=10$, $Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}$. Consequently $Q(6)=11.4501\\ldots\\,\\mathrm{kg}$, or $11.45\\,\\mathrm{kg}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64b2a1d5-c1ff-542b-b36c-6db0efde0080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.17\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377244} {:answer/id #uuid "1e022231-3289-56f5-b77a-6fa5e2768d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.96\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377245} {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} {:answer/id #uuid "3a37a27b-7d8d-5e83-886d-a22fd90988bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.02\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377247} {:answer/id #uuid "974b466b-a863-5b62-b0b7-196c75665716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.83\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339b8286-0b9a-5bce-8b4a-139dcdcd79c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.49\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377246} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e36df41-d08a-5164-b00e-7830a62b1c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377243}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea5d5e7-af5c-553a-9742-7d54f2384984" :question/math-academy-id "q-336734" :question/problem "A nutrient-mixing tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of solution containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of supplement in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which supplements enter the tank minus the rate at which supplements leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which supplements enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the supplement enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *supplement* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which supplements exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of supplement in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *supplement* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved supplement. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+21.6 & =3 \\\\ A & =-18.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of supplement in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=21.6-18.6{e}^{-5/60}≈4.49\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6b3af3-595e-5f2a-9b02-bcc7246f44a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-5{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377258} {:answer/id #uuid "13d461e2-b966-5ab9-b642-de837c2fca9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377259} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed3fa03-0898-5985-81d9-f0e37cb5e0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+16{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377261} {:answer/id #uuid "570d8df2-e03e-5a30-8e43-d41eb6518c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-2{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0d6e4d-2317-5060-be03-4689c9b28f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a573fd39-4b38-5a00-b6f4-d30c1ee02cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55a40904-7261-506e-bb45-5a9e0fad5627" :question/math-academy-id "q-336750" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved anti-corrosion additive.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $500\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+10 & =7 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10-3{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d2fe13-097f-5e6f-9c33-2c32b0e27d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10-7{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377265} {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd3ac0f-6dcc-58e2-95b1-2de9c477d9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-1+4{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377267} {:answer/id #uuid "864fcaca-7914-585c-ab8c-d6bf235cc102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=4-{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377268} {:answer/id #uuid "c03d0083-ace6-59b4-99bc-ac215dea614c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-9+12{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed6b381a-a203-5376-a746-2de328b0ebe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9149b0d8-97dc-543b-a8ef-8e274f2d81bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e940f8bd-b1c9-5052-8127-7c721b39ac69" :question/math-academy-id "q-336753" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $3\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=3,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =3 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =3 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-9{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f7f8d63-3c73-54e5-a321-064e59a6e495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377281} {:answer/id #uuid "936ab165-4ff7-5e7c-b165-a792940b2983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377282} {:answer/id #uuid "933d636b-7f9a-5a8d-877e-255718054cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=2-9{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377283} {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f664fa-8d20-5d06-954d-374ce7cf36e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9+7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e658c55e-dd94-5640-bd43-2b24b743ccb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377284} :answer-field/id #uuid "66547b5b-bb7a-5943-a4eb-d13453f82fe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377280}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36b5068a-10fd-5970-abe3-44c13a56330c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339382" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $450\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $450\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{15}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{15}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{15}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=9.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/15}+9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/15}+9 & =2 \\\\ A & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=9-7{e}^{-t/15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495231bb-9e61-56b4-8093-d154d6a40880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.32\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377288} {:answer/id #uuid "4dec0265-b5b6-5491-9040-ac9785af058c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377289} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ed57a2-b88d-543a-a90d-9bbd77f9672a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.98\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377290} {:answer/id #uuid "53545ea8-a0a8-5fac-8720-960a278e9b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.54\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377291} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd1d53-820b-5967-911f-26f348052b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377292} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e0f293-e28a-5788-b1b2-c59142094521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377287}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f9ca797-1282-5c7a-958a-6e7a8b023c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339390" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additives enter the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=8,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =8 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+7 & =8 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=7+{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of sugar in the tank $4$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(4)=7+{e}^{-4/14}≈7.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012bbc31-fce8-5143-bbdb-f134d11c9b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25-15{e}^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501467} {:answer/id #uuid "31248321-fb90-5cb0-aa5a-920f043c50d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-20+30{e}^{t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501553} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed776e2-31c7-5d6e-9bbc-6f6a10752d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=30-20{e}^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501578} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8cf9da-4b94-562b-ad43-2abfa2d2e1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=-2+12{e}^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501663} {:answer/id #uuid "a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2495ea6-828d-5486-8e26-3bd05d9010fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501777} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda88114-20cf-5991-bcbf-f77da4f62dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336752" :question/problem "A mixing tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved cleanser is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of cleanser $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501473} {:answer/id #uuid "8a602200-48ad-53f0-8143-6fe9792d4181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501731} {:answer/id #uuid "d5adba53-2823-56b8-b86a-d86b90f0927a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.32\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501890} {:answer/id #uuid "da3ec5e9-ce8a-5063-b326-988d95b5ddd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501903} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc5fe1b-7116-5783-a523-4a7779acf88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04076803-eae5-51dd-adf5-ffaa457974c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.75\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501473} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f84c734-a5ae-5ecb-9eb0-7a39a117167f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-336736" :question/problem "A mixing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f66fb4-40cf-56c6-b199-8c73d618fc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501503} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501609} {:answer/id #uuid "578ba4bb-a618-5ab5-8b97-78550b74205b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12+11{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501628} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8273dc-02bc-5c58-83c8-72b74f957f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=1-12{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501842} {:answer/id #uuid "c413a31a-8d21-5344-817f-3bab2b829337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f46af42-cc7d-5f1f-afb2-f63457bfad29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501609} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdf6499-b332-5612-bc05-cf92d925d07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63623e2d-9a1a-53b1-a242-66f71be1ef8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339383" :question/problem "A pharmaceutical mixer initially holds $300\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved medication is pumped into the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of medication $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the mixer $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074afea5-fe43-5f97-a337-7ba0b62f6b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.19\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557576} {:answer/id #uuid "3a34b986-628b-586a-a824-0c50dc501cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48.86\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557589} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2bd5f4-6495-521f-86b4-02f859db90d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557602} {:answer/id #uuid "abf2cb4e-eaec-5478-bf8c-311b280dee6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.53\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557611} {:answer/id #uuid "c8adf232-a644-562e-a8a4-c1c1bb1a72e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2bd5f4-6495-521f-86b4-02f859db90d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557602} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf78f28-d615-5f34-b6af-c0a3d54ad644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f49ce74-136e-52d1-86ad-43f235e9acf3" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $560\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $60\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e46878-fee9-50ca-98ed-c5b7828d3a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.84\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557601} {:answer/id #uuid "cf838e6d-1539-51ea-a41d-a097421083cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.80\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557616} {:answer/id #uuid "dc34f15f-1945-5fa3-8396-6a623c0a9b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557620} {:answer/id #uuid "f1df0184-1f89-5672-8d0f-455e36d369e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557622} {:answer/id #uuid "fca55ac1-6715-59f4-9c7b-dcca6d1300f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.87\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc34f15f-1945-5fa3-8396-6a623c0a9b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557620} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd3035f-bf25-5206-aea4-72cd5a9c6c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166e03f1-5265-5464-911b-78beb96ade57" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=54.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+54.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+54 & =15 \\\\ A & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=54-39{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=54-39{e}^{-5/60}≈18.12\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32982bee-e49c-5d3c-9ef9-9797891a4a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.80\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557586} {:answer/id #uuid "4db1f4c8-9663-5522-85a7-ac3f6dbb587a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557594} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb7d840-bfd4-5d23-a40a-54e9d62eb633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557603} {:answer/id #uuid "8c234248-4412-52b3-a150-1ca22bcf39ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.87\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557607} {:answer/id #uuid "d70ad977-02f6-5ad7-a6df-586c35f0ecd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.84\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4db1f4c8-9663-5522-85a7-ac3f6dbb587a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557594} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0d8da9-229e-5e9e-b1b3-e1ac54f4e6c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557604}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25f7192c-e0d8-5fe5-81fc-4a226077ebf0" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $60\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=54.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+54.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+54 & =15 \\\\ A & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=54-39{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=54-39{e}^{-5/60}≈18.12\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1d1dad-63e4-53b7-84e8-513185e6d304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.19\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557582} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6bca90-9a03-5e51-b918-70f707c6acd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557583} {:answer/id #uuid "3519affe-7179-51a9-9bfa-a2371788e71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557587} {:answer/id #uuid "41d54f50-31ef-5861-8452-ba4837b9997d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.53\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557592} {:answer/id #uuid "97fcfedc-afd7-5fbd-b34c-7c2e83880c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48.86\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6bca90-9a03-5e51-b918-70f707c6acd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557583} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc00a72d-8bd3-57d1-9224-3683c4e00277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557623}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "296bf566-e278-5d2e-983b-7265ee8fce14" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $560\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecd4a70-5d43-5b6e-b51f-7860ae3003f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.48\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557591} {:answer/id #uuid "53fa8896-959f-5e2c-bef6-40a7e49e0d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.29\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557595} {:answer/id #uuid "7505055c-ebf8-59d8-95d5-613b69c1b664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.72\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557600} {:answer/id #uuid "802b7168-0e5a-518c-9174-74d6fc00f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.16\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557605} {:answer/id #uuid "c754b326-0708-5675-b4f9-bc4175d982b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7505055c-ebf8-59d8-95d5-613b69c1b664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.72\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557600} :answer-field/id #uuid "e946d965-efc2-5c52-804f-d33d0dbc4425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557621}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45703f0e-7f20-5a04-876b-6489f25e25bd" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $24\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $30\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of additive in the tank $3$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 30\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{24}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{24}Q=\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{24}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/24},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{24}\\alpha =\\frac{9}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/24}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/24}+21.6 & =24 \\\\ A & =2.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6+2.4{e}^{-t/24}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of additive in the tank $3$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(3)=21.6+2.4{e}^{-3/24}≈23.72\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb044d0-3b14-5394-8670-15805bd24f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.77\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557579} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcc7f0d-605a-57f5-a1d3-53972e15f66d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.39\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557585} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e95dc-9cb4-5ea0-9b47-f9315553f561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.62\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557590} {:answer/id #uuid "83a9bfca-6560-5ddc-9727-d1da746b19e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.27\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557606} {:answer/id #uuid "b20b695b-e151-57d5-aa83-88aa35a655c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.23\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20b695b-e151-57d5-aa83-88aa35a655c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.23\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557612} :answer-field/id #uuid "9691fa90-f342-5762-97c2-78f160bf5d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66ef737b-6897-576f-afdf-4557492d2e08" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $625\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $13\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $30\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of additive in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 30\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $625\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.75.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.75\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =18.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=18.75.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+18.75.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $13\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=13,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =13 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+18.75 & =13 \\\\ A & =-5.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18.75-5.75{e}^{-t/25}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of additive in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(6)=18.75-5.75{e}^{-6/25}≈14.23\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eab0c72-a9b6-5914-a862-8b14af356b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.68\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557584} {:answer/id #uuid "36655816-75f6-5463-8b00-0f2116bceb0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.73\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557588} {:answer/id #uuid "71feb9bd-395e-5d1e-a142-a57c5b2eba4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.05\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557599} {:answer/id #uuid "d557c772-fa39-54f8-84d9-854c4e0af932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45.95\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e97afd-028a-57f8-b966-b25ee6c663ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.29\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71feb9bd-395e-5d1e-a142-a57c5b2eba4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.05\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557599} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb724058-9e20-5567-9da6-1e400ea9f683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9970f4fb-cac3-5bd9-8a36-546b0cf184df" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $40\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $6$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 40\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=36.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+36.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+36 & =25 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=36-11{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $6$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(6)=36-11{e}^{-6/60}≈26.05\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247aea16-5a0e-53c2-9eab-769593e0321b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-4.8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} {:answer/id #uuid "4a75fa10-6da7-5407-9c8d-f142fc65c9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672848} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf6c63e-69eb-5222-be8b-10c3f1be8e3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672850} {:answer/id #uuid "9294db4d-c049-5c7e-9775-3e4ab32bdfb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-0.8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672860} {:answer/id #uuid "d583b770-9481-5579-8d91-684112cd4467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-12e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "247aea16-5a0e-53c2-9eab-769593e0321b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-4.8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72c24e8-2705-5501-b569-59c87bcb3ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b80a0f4-7801-535e-bc20-eae6ddee5773" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $360\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $360\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{30}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30}+10.8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30}+10.8 & =6 \\\\ A & =-4.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10.8-4.8{e}^{-t/30}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2672e6b2-eafa-5b83-9c60-d31c4e3baaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=14-2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672843} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6606fe-295f-54d9-a96b-ece95eaa67cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672852} {:answer/id #uuid "a57aeafe-7041-5c00-8fd6-c39c93acb22f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=14+4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672863} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7feec-a4a9-5925-8994-9b16f6261677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} {:answer/id #uuid "f524c191-98bd-54bf-8c61-18cfacadfeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-9e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7feec-a4a9-5925-8994-9b16f6261677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} :answer-field/id #uuid "669474bd-f365-5bcf-8161-9f793e84c972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "387eb92e-520a-5684-94a0-8f6b898f8d01" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =1.5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+21.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+21 & =18 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21-3{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a120c58-a242-55df-ae6b-10b3