\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.25\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $1000\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.25-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.25\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+10 & =15 \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10+5{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} {:answer/id #uuid "67f70dc6-1daf-5923-90ad-76fd65eed828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672855} {:answer/id #uuid "996a9731-acc5-5b69-8d22-f2fd2ac623d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672861} {:answer/id #uuid "ae638f6f-9aab-5257-8412-79161e72772d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-3.4e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672865} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dc6910-635d-50c4-b176-6fbb2ac614b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-21.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} :answer-field/id #uuid "23514375-6fae-594a-b5a7-0e55f8d76eee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672841}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3e93811-bde2-50b4-a162-641048dbfc47" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $420\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =8.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=8.4.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+8.4.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+8.4 & =4 \\\\ A & =-4.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=8.4-4.4{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model: Inflow Equals Outflow"}]}
